; Crisis management. Winning 42 p. 50 (S-0073-d), pp. 92-96 (S-0123-d,
; S-0123-e, S-0124-a, S-0127-a), pp. 106-108 (S-0139-a); played in Hand 26
; (the second line) and Hand 31 (trick 2).
; Rule, in our words: when a setter turns out to hold trump the bidder cannot
; draw, the bidder stops drawing and works around it for count while the trump
; holder must still follow. Holding the 6-5 and the 6-4, neither trump, he
; leads the 6-5 as bait: a setter void in sixes may waste a trump on it,
; fearing the 6-4 is elsewhere, and the partner learns where the 6-4 is. Else
; he cashes a double that is itself count (the 5-5); else a double that calls
; for count, away from a suit the trump holder has followed (two to one that
; tile was his only one, and he could trump the double); else any double that
; calls for count, the one whose suit has the most tiles still out.
; Replaces gates/c14-crisis-cash-the-double.silas and
; gates/c21-six-five-bait.silas. How it differs: C14's trigger is exactly one
; trump trick in which one opponent followed; the book's crises come after two
; trump tricks (Hand 26), when a trump the bidder wants was not drawn (Hand
; 27), or as an off lead (Hand 31). C14 allows every non-trump double,
; including one that calls for no count (the 1-1 the book calls worthless in
; Hand 26), and lacks the count-at-stake first choice and the override. C21
; fires on any lead; the book plays the bait as a crisis lead (its trick-one
; 6-5 off of p. 132 is first-lead.silas's question).
; GAP aggregate: "the suit with the most tiles out" compares two sets' sizes;
; the last two rules allow every count-calling double.
; GAP likelihood: in Hand 31 the crisis itself is a reading of the right
; setter's count play (prelude, trumps-he-cannot-draw).

